## 3. Why side sixteen resolves the topology problem

The PRE's physical flat limit first integrates the gradient and harmonic angle fibers. For the same final matter word, two Laurent shifts interfere only if their difference has zero divergence and zero harmonic signature. A field packet with zero electric winding does not localize harmonic angles at zero. The PRE's side-six and side-eight comparators explicitly show that dropping this integration can change a second moment.

The released note instead fixes every period to sixteen and bounds every Laurent word used in its leading coefficients. This is sufficient. If k is a circulation on this torus, its three oriented coordinate sums are \(16w_\mu\), with integer winding numbers \(w_\mu\). Thus

\[
 16\sum_\mu|w_\mu|\le\sum_e|k_e|.
\]

A circulation of word length less than sixteen has zero winding. It need not be a single elementary plaquette: zero winding is the needed assertion for the Haar average, and at the transverse origin every remaining character equals one.

For the prepared two-branch expectation, the common electric string cancels from the angle-multiplier tests. Relative branch strings add at most two increments. H4 contributes at most four per monomial and each B at most two. The lengths used by the author are consequently correct:

| Prepared test | Maximum circulation length |
|---|---:|
| \(J^*H_4J\) | 6 |
| \(J^*H_4^2J\) | 10 |
| \(J^*B^*BH_4J\), or its adjoint | 10 |
| \(J^*H_4B^*BH_4J\) | 14 |

The full Gauss-preserving operators and the branch dressings make each scalar term a circulation; this is why the matter-only calculation can be used at this final step. For a definite normalized output word, the fixed output string cancels. The diagonal H4 and H4-squared tests have lengths at most four/eight. The weight \(|W_p-I|^2=2-W_p-W_p^*\) adds at most four, so their weighted lengths are at most eight/twelve. These are also below sixteen.

This justifies the author's finite low-moment flat coefficients. It does not justify putting \(e^{-irH_4}\) at zero angles at all orders, nor dropping D. The critical-window proof uses a bounded Taylor remainder for the full magnetic operator and separately controls D, so it does not need that stronger assertion.

The side-six rows in the author control are accurately identified as flat-matter comparators. They agree with the PRE's *incorrect-for-that-physical-limit all-zero-angle comparator*, not with its harmonic-averaged side-six result. No unfavorable row is suppressed.

