## 6. Count variance, restricted optimization and microscopic order

The bounded marked intensity gives

\[
 \mathbb E[N_j(N_j-1)]\le (\kappa\|B_j\|^2)^2b_g^2.
\]

Since the leading mean is positive and of order \(\kappa b_g g^2\), the relative factorial correction is \(O(\kappa b_g/g^2)=O(g)\). Hence \(\operatorname{Var}_n(N_j)/m_n\to1\). This is the same argument as the PRE; it does not impose Poisson statistics or exclude other marks.

For N independent supplied preparations in each arm and \(\chi_p\ne0\), the root's asymptotic coefficient is

\[
 \mathrm{SNR}^2\sim N\kappa\tau g^5|\chi_p|^4
 \frac{\beta}{A+\beta^2L/24},\qquad A=v_p+|\chi_p|^2>0.
\]

Differentiating the last fraction gives a derivative proportional to \(A-\beta^2L/24\). For \(L>0\) the unique positive maximizer is

\[
 \beta_* =\sqrt{24A/L},\qquad
 \max_{\beta>0}\frac{\beta}{A+\beta^2L/24}=\frac{\sqrt6}{\sqrt{AL}}.
\]

The algebra is correct. For this fixed-beta family, order-\(g^{-5}\) supplied repetitions with a sufficiently large constant yields the stated Chebyshev sign-error control. This is optimization of the limiting coefficient over fixed positive beta. Uniform remainders in a g-dependent or unbounded beta family are not supplied, so a more general finite-g or all-protocol optimum does not follow. The source explicitly excludes preparation, timing, energy spread, resets and microscopic spin resources from that count.

The last sentence about microscopic transfer needs the accepted limit-order correction. At fixed physical g and b, a finite time mesh permits the existing finite-register microscopic convergence as \(S\to\infty\). Target bounded intensity controls the endpoint-bin error as the mesh tends to zero; the finite physical birth cap also bounds first and second count moments. Equivalently, for a desired row tolerance, first choose a target mesh with sufficiently small target boundary error and then choose spin above the convergence threshold for that mesh. A later g-dependent diagonal can be chosen to meet a relative tolerance, but no quantitative spin rate or arbitrary simultaneous schedule follows. There is no uniform microscopic point-density statement allowing a fixed finite spin to support all refinements.

